V=[
X(1,'THE AUSPICIOUS ONE',LS('Lalita seated on a jewelled mountain peak','KALYĀṆĪ','mountain'),
 'She whose form is the syllable ka; the auspicious one; rich in auspicious qualities; who dwells on the auspicious mountain; the lovely one; the one full of arts.',
 [('kakārarūpā','<b>whose form is the syllable ka</b>'),('kalyāṇī','<b>the auspicious</b>'),('kalyāṇaguṇaśālinī','<b>endowed with auspicious qualities</b>'),('kalyāṇaśailanilayā','<b>dwelling on the auspicious mountain</b>'),('kamanīyā','<b>desirable, lovely</b>'),('kalāvatī','<b>possessing the arts</b>')],
 'The Triśatī belongs to the Lalitopākhyāna, the story of Lalitā in the Brahmāṇḍa Purāṇa, told as a dialogue between Hayagrīva, Viṣṇu’s horse-headed teacher form, and the sage Agastya. Agastya had already received the Lalitā Sahasranāma and still felt something missing; these 300 names are the answer. They fall in fifteen groups of twenty names, each group opening with one syllable of a mantra given at initiation in the Śrīvidyā tradition. We do not assemble or explain that mantra here.',
 'The first name makes the method plain: the Goddess is the sound itself, not merely praised by it. Kalyāṇa, auspicious, repeats three times, as if the hymn needed to settle the mind in goodness before anything else. The “auspicious mountain” is read as Meru, on whose summit the Śrīpura, Lalitā’s city, stands, or as the Śrīcakra’s raised form. Kalāvatī joins the arts to her: the sixty-four arts and the sixteen phases (kalā) of the moon.',
 'Begin anything important by fixing the mind on what is good. The rest follows more easily.',
 'The hymn begins in <b>auspiciousness</b>.'),
X(2,'THE LOTUS-EYED',LS('Lalita in a grove of kadamba trees','KADAMBA','tree'),
 'The lotus-eyed; destroyer of impurity; ocean of the nectar of compassion; who lives in the kadamba forest; who loves kadamba flowers.',
 [('kamalākṣī','<b>lotus-eyed</b>'),('kalmaṣaghnī','<b>destroyer of sin and impurity</b>'),('karuṇāmṛtasāgarā','<b>ocean of the nectar of compassion</b>'),('kadambakānanāvāsā','<b>dwelling in the kadamba forest</b>'),('kadambakusumapriyā','<b>fond of kadamba flowers</b>')],
 'The kadamba forest (kadambavana) is a fixed feature of Lalitā’s world. In the Lalitopākhyāna her city sits within rings of walls and groves, and the innermost grove is of kadamba trees. The Madurai Mīnākṣī temple, by tradition, stands where a kadamba forest once grew, and Mīnākṣī is often identified with Lalitā.',
 'Two names here describe what she does and three describe where and how she is. The sequence is gentle: her eyes, then her mercy, then her home. Kalmaṣa means stain, sin or dullness; kalmaṣaghnī, she who kills it, appears at the very start so the reader is reassured before going deeper. Commentators often note that compassion is called nectar, amṛta, because it heals and does not run out.',
 'Mercy that does not run out is a good thing to keep in mind when dealing with others too.',
 'An <b>ocean</b> of compassion.'),
X(3,'THE SCIENCE OF KĀMA',LS('Lalita glancing sideways, a small figure of Kama with a bow appearing','KANDARPA','heart'),
 'She who is the knowledge of Kandarpa; whose side-glance gave birth to Kandarpa; who fills the directions with waves of fragrance from the camphor betel she chews.',
 [('kandarpavidyā','<b>the knowledge (vidyā) of Kandarpa</b>'),('kandarpajanakāpāṅgavīkṣaṇā','<b>whose sidelong glance gave birth to Kāma</b>'),('karpūravīṭī…','<b>camphor-betel</b>'),('…saurabhyakallolitakakuptaṭā','<b>whose fragrance flows in waves to the ends of the directions</b>')],
 'Kandarpa is Kāma, the god of love. In the Lalitopākhyāna, Kāma was burned to ash by Śiva’s third eye; from the ash rose the demon Bhaṇḍa, and the gods prayed to the Goddess. Lalitā appeared from the fire of their sacrifice, destroyed Bhaṇḍa, and with a glance brought Kāma back to life. That is the story behind this name.',
 'Kandarpavidyā is a technical name: the Śrīvidyā mantra is said to have been first worshipped by Kāma, and one of its traditional forms is called the Kādi vidyā after him. So the Goddess is both the one who revives love and the knowledge love itself worships. The camphor-betel name is pure delight: a queenly detail of scent, as if her presence could be smelled in every direction.',
 'Love that was destroyed can be restored by grace. Do not give up on it too soon.',
 'Her glance <b>revived love</b>.'),
X(4,'WITNESS OF ACTION',LS('Lalita watching people at work, a lamp in the centre','SĀKṢIṆĪ','lamp'),
 'She who removes the faults of the Kali age; the lotus-eyed; of lovely form; the witness of action and all else; who causes action; who gives the fruits of action.',
 [('kalidoṣaharā','<b>remover of the Kali age’s faults</b>'),('kañjalocanā','<b>lotus-eyed</b>'),('kamravigrahā','<b>of lovely form</b>'),('karmādisākṣiṇī','<b>witness of action and the rest</b>'),('kārayitrī','<b>who causes to act</b>'),('karmaphalapradā','<b>giver of the fruit of action</b>')],
 'These names close the first group of twenty. They move from beauty to karma: she watches every act, moves the actor, and returns the result. The ideas echo the Gītā, where the Lord is the witness (upadraṣṭā) and the giver of results, and Kṛṣṇa says he sits in every heart, moving all beings.',
 'Kali, the present age of the world, is marked by strife and confusion; a deity who removes its faults is especially sought in it. The three karma names together describe the whole cycle: sākṣiṇī, the witness who sees; kārayitrī, the inner mover; and karmaphalapradā, the dispenser of results. Some commentators read “karmādi” as “action, knowledge and devotion”, all of which she witnesses.',
 'Act as though you are seen, because, the texts say, you are.',
 'She <b>sees, moves and repays</b> every act.'),
X(5,'BEYOND “THIS” AND “THAT”',LS('Lalita above a single glowing syllable and many letters','EKĀKṢARĪ','om'),
 'She whose form is the syllable e; the single syllable; whose form is one syllable and many; who cannot be pointed out as “this” or “that”; whose form is one bliss and consciousness.',
 [('ekārarūpā','<b>whose form is the syllable e</b>'),('ekākṣarī','<b>the one syllable</b>'),('ekānekākṣarākṛtiḥ','<b>formed of one and many syllables</b>'),('etattadityanirdeśyā','<b>not to be indicated as “this” or “that”</b>'),('ekānandacidākṛtiḥ','<b>whose form is one bliss and consciousness</b>')],
 'A new group of twenty begins, and its names almost all start with eka, “one”: a deliberate play on the syllable that opens the group. The theme moves from her beauty to her nature as the one reality.',
 'Ekākṣarī, the single syllable, is often read as Om; the “one and many syllables” are all of language, which comes from it. “Not this, not that” echoes the Upaniṣadic neti neti: the highest cannot be pointed at like an object. Ekānandacidākṛti, one bliss and consciousness, is the Vedāntic description of Brahman. Śrīvidyā holds that Lalitā is that Brahman with form, personal and beyond form at once.',
 'Some things can only be known by becoming quiet, not by pointing.',
 '<b>One</b> syllable, all of language.'),
X(6,'KNOWN BY ONE-POINTED DEVOTION',LS('A devotee meditating with one-pointed focus before Lalita','EKĀGRA','light'),
 'She who cannot be known by the scriptures saying “thus”; worshipped by single-minded devotion; meditated on by the one-pointed mind; honoured by those free of cravings.',
 [('evamityāgamābodhyā','<b>not knowable by scripture saying “it is thus”</b>'),('ekabhaktimadarcitā','<b>worshipped by those of single devotion</b>'),('ekāgracittanirdhyātā','<b>meditated on by the one-pointed mind</b>'),('eṣaṇārahitādṛtā','<b>cherished by those without cravings</b>')],
 'This verse gives a short course in how she is reached. Scripture alone cannot capture her; only devotion, concentration and freedom from craving can.',
 'Āgama means the scriptures, especially the Tantras. To say she cannot be known by them “thus” is not to reject them, but to say that words point and experience arrives. The eṣaṇās, “cravings”, are classically three: for wealth, for children and for fame, named in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad as what renunciants give up. Ekāgra, one-pointed, is Patañjali’s word for the concentrated mind.',
 'Focus is a form of worship. A scattered mind can read everything and see nothing.',
 'Found by <b>one-pointed</b> love.'),
X(7,'FRAGRANT HAIR, ONE TASTE',LS('Lalita with flowing fragrant hair, dark clouds of sin breaking','EKARASĀ','star'),
 'She whose hair is fragrant with cardamom; destroyer of heaps of sin; the one enjoyer; of one essence; giver of sole sovereignty.',
 [('elāsugandhicikurā','<b>whose hair is fragrant with cardamom</b>'),('enaḥkūṭavināśinī','<b>destroyer of heaps of sin</b>'),('ekabhogā','<b>the one enjoyer</b>'),('ekarasā','<b>of one taste, one essence</b>'),('ekaiśvaryapradāyinī','<b>giver of undivided sovereignty</b>')],
 'Lalitā hymns move freely from the body of the Goddess to philosophy and back. Here a detail of fragrance sits beside a statement about the one essence of reality.',
 'Enas is an old Vedic word for sin; kūṭa, a heap or mass. Ekabhoga, the one enjoyer, says that all experience is finally hers: she alone experiences through every being. Ekarasa, one taste, is a classic Vedāntic image: salt dissolved in water gives the whole of it one taste. Ekaiśvarya, sole lordship, is read both as worldly rule and as the inner sovereignty of the realised.',
 'Sweetness of presence and depth of mind belong together. Cultivate both.',
 'All of life has <b>one taste</b>: hers.'),
X(8,'THE SINGLE ROYAL UMBRELLA',LS('Lalita under a single white royal parasol, many worlds around','EKĀTAPATRA','crown'),
 'Giver of empire under a single royal umbrella; worshipped by the exclusive devotee; of ever-growing radiance; ruler of the many worlds, moving and still.',
 [('ekātapatrasāmrājyapradā','<b>giver of an empire under one umbrella</b>'),('ekāntapūjitā','<b>worshipped in solitude, by the exclusive</b>'),('edhamānaprabhā','<b>of increasing radiance</b>'),('ejadanekajagadīśvarī','<b>ruler of countless worlds, moving and unmoving</b>')],
 'The ātapatra, the white umbrella held over a king, was the sign of sovereignty in ancient India; one umbrella over the whole land meant undisputed rule. Lalitā is the Empress, Śrī Mahārājñī, and these names are royal.',
 'Ekānta has two senses: “in solitude” and “exclusive”, so ekāntapūjitā is she who is worshipped in private, with whole devotion. Edhamāna, ever-increasing, reverses ordinary experience, where brightness fades. Ejat means “moving”; the worlds she rules include all that moves and all that does not, a phrase from the Īśa Upaniṣad’s opening.',
 'Real authority grows brighter with time when it serves rightly.',
 'Empress of <b>countless worlds</b>.'),
X(9,'THE HEROES’ GODDESS',LS('Warriors bowing before Lalita, a ray of light from her','EKAVĪRA','sword'),
 'She who is served by Ekavīra and others; of unique majesty. She whose form is the syllable ī; the ruler; giver of desired things.',
 [('ekavīrādisaṃsevyā','<b>served by Ekavīra and other heroes</b>'),('ekaprābhavaśālinī','<b>of unequalled power</b>'),('īkārarūpā','<b>whose form is the syllable ī</b>'),('īśitrī','<b>the ruler</b>'),('īpsitārthapradāyinī','<b>giver of what is wished for</b>')],
 'The group of e-names ends and the ī-names begin in the middle of this verse. Ekavīra is read as a great hero, or as a name of Śiva or of Dattātreya; in the Lalitopākhyāna, heroic goddesses and gods serve her in the war against Bhaṇḍa.',
 'Prābhava, majesty or power, is “eka”, unique: there is none like it. The new group of ī-names will be dominated by īśa, lordship, and the relationship of the Goddess to Īśvara, Śiva. Īpsitārtha, the wished-for thing, recurs in many forms through the Triśatī: these names are also used in worship for specific needs.',
 'Ask for what you need, plainly. The hymn does.',
 'The heroes’ <b>queen</b>.'),
X(10,'GIVER OF THE EIGHT POWERS',LS('Lalita above Brahma, Vishnu, Rudra and Ishana','AṢṬASIDDHI','gods'),
 'She who cannot be described as “such”; who confers lordship; who is Īśāna and the other Brahmans; giver of the eight powers beginning with lordship.',
 [('īdṛgityavinirdeśyā','<b>not describable as “like this”</b>'),('īśvaratvavidhāyinī','<b>who bestows lordship</b>'),('īśānādibrahmamayī','<b>made of Īśāna and the other forms of Brahman</b>'),('īśitvādyaṣṭasiddhidā','<b>giver of the eight powers, lordship and the rest</b>')],
 'Īśāna is one of the five faces of Śiva and also one of the “five Brahmans” (pañca brahma) that in Śrīvidyā imagery form the couch on which Lalitā sits: Brahmā, Viṣṇu, Rudra and Īśvara as its legs, Sadāśiva as its plank.',
 'The idea is striking: the great gods are her furniture. They serve as the seat of the Goddess, so she is said to give them their lordship (īśvaratva). The eight siddhis, aṇimā and the rest, are the classical yogic powers; īśitva, lordship, is one of them. Śrīvidyā teachers usually warn that these powers are by-products, not goals.',
 'Power is given, not owned. Use it as something on loan.',
 'Even the gods are her <b>seat</b>.'),
X(11,'WORLDS FROM A GLANCE',LS('Lalita’s glance creating many small eggs of worlds','ĪKṢAṆA','star'),
 'She who by her glance creates crores of universes; beloved of Īśvara; the praised one; she who is half of Īśvara’s body; the presiding deity of Īśa.',
 [('īkṣitrīkṣaṇasṛṣṭāṇḍakoṭiḥ','<b>who creates crores of world-eggs by a glance</b>'),('īśvaravallabhā','<b>beloved of Īśvara</b>'),('īḍitā','<b>the praised</b>'),('īśvarārdhāṅgaśarīrā','<b>whose body is half of Īśvara’s</b>'),('īśādhidevatā','<b>the deity over Īśa</b>')],
 'The Chāndogya Upaniṣad says that Being “looked” (aikṣata) and thought “may I be many”, and creation followed. This name gives that creative glance to the Goddess: worlds arise as she looks.',
 'Īśvarārdhāṅgaśarīrā is the Ardhanārīśvara image: Śiva and Śakti as one body, half male and half female. Īśādhidevatā is a bold title: the deity who presides even over the Lord. Śrīvidyā’s theology makes Śiva and Śakti inseparable but gives Śakti the active role: without her, says a famous verse of the Saundaryalaharī, Śiva cannot even stir.',
 'Creation begins with attention. What you look at, you help bring into being.',
 'Worlds arise from her <b>glance</b>.'),
X(12,'WITNESS OF ŚIVA’S DANCE',LS('Lalita watching Shiva dance','TĀṆḌAVA','fire'),
 'She who inspires Īśvara; witness of Īśa’s tāṇḍava dance; who rests in Īśvara’s lap; destroyer of afflictions.',
 [('īśvarapreraṇakarī','<b>who impels Īśvara</b>'),('īśatāṇḍavasākṣiṇī','<b>witness of the Lord’s cosmic dance</b>'),('īśvarotsaṅganilayā','<b>who dwells on the Lord’s lap</b>'),('ītibādhāvināśinī','<b>destroyer of calamities and afflictions</b>')],
 'At the end of each world-age Śiva dances the tāṇḍava, the dance of dissolution, and everything is drawn back into him. Śrīvidyā tradition says only the Goddess remains to witness it, and from her the next creation comes.',
 'Ītis are traditionally six calamities of farming life: excessive rain, drought, locusts, rats, parrots and foreign invasion. A goddess who destroys them is a very practical deity. The image of Lalitā on Śiva’s lap (utsaṅga) is the standard iconography of Kāmeśvara and Kāmeśvarī, the divine couple at the centre of the Śrīcakra.',
 'Even at the end of things, someone keeps watch. Endings are not the last word.',
 'She <b>watches</b> the dance of endings.'),
X(13,'LALITĀ',LS('Lalita smiling, Lakshmi and Sarasvati fanning her','LALITĀ','heart'),
 'She who is free of craving; the power of Īśa; whose face has a gentle smile. She whose form is the syllable la; Lalitā; served by Lakṣmī and Vāṇī.',
 [('īhāvirahitā','<b>free from desire</b>'),('īśaśaktiḥ','<b>the power of the Lord</b>'),('īṣatsmitānanā','<b>whose face has a slight smile</b>'),('lakārarūpā','<b>whose form is the syllable la</b>'),('lalitā','<b>the playful, the lovely</b>'),('lakṣmīvāṇīniṣevitā','<b>served by Lakṣmī and Sarasvatī</b>')],
 'Halfway through this verse the group of ī-names ends and the la-group begins, and its first true name is Lalitā itself. The same Goddess is called Tripurasundarī, the beauty of the three cities, and Rājarājeśvarī, queen of kings.',
 'Lalita means playful, graceful, charming. Commentators explain it as play: creation is her līlā, done without need, as a child plays. The slight smile (īṣatsmita) is her signature in hymns. That Lakṣmī and Sarasvatī attend her shows her above the goddesses of wealth and learning, in the same way that the great gods form her seat.',
 'Lightness is not shallowness. Grace can carry great power.',
 '<b>Lalitā</b>: the one who plays.'),
X(14,'THE POMEGRANATE-HUED',LS('Lalita in rosy pomegranate colour with a forehead jewel','PĀṬALĀ','crown'),
 'Lākinī; whose form is womanhood; rosy as a bright pomegranate flower; whose forehead shines with a jewelled ornament; worshipped by the three-eyed Śiva.',
 [('lākinī','<b>Lākinī, the goddess of the navel centre</b>'),('lalanārūpā','<b>whose form is woman</b>'),('lasaddāḍimapāṭalā','<b>rosy as the shining pomegranate</b>'),('lalantikālasatphālā','<b>forehead shining with a pendant</b>'),('lalāṭanayanārcitā','<b>worshipped by the forehead-eyed (Śiva)</b>')],
 'Lākinī is one of the yoginīs who preside over the bodily cakras in Lalitā’s worship; she is linked with the maṇipūra, the navel centre. Such names connect the hymn with inner yoga as well as outer worship.',
 'Lalanārūpā, “whose form is woman”, is read in two ways: she appears as a woman, and every woman is her form. Śākta traditions draw from this a strong teaching of reverence for women. Her colour is constantly described as red or rosy: dawn, pomegranate blossom, saffron. The lalantikā is a pendant ornament that hangs on the forehead.',
 'See the Goddess in the women of your own life, and treat them so.',
 'In every <b>woman</b>, her form.'),
X(15,'BEYOND ALL MARKS',LS('Lalita, slender as a creeper, galaxies behind her','LAKṢAṆA','star'),
 'Whose divine limbs glow with auspicious marks; ruler of a hundred thousand crore universes; the goal to be aimed at; unreachable by marks; whose desires are fulfilled; slender as a creeper.',
 [('lakṣaṇojjvaladivyāṅgī','<b>whose divine body shines with auspicious marks</b>'),('lakṣakoṭyaṇḍanāyikā','<b>mistress of countless universes</b>'),('lakṣyārthā','<b>the meaning to be aimed at</b>'),('lakṣaṇāgamyā','<b>not reached by definition</b>'),('labdhakāmā','<b>whose desires are fulfilled</b>'),('latātanuḥ','<b>slender as a creeper</b>')],
 'Here the poet plays on lakṣaṇa: a mark, a definition, an auspicious sign. Her body has every auspicious mark, yet she cannot be reached by marks or definitions.',
 'Lakṣyārthā is a technical term from Indian semantics: lakṣaṇā is the secondary or implied meaning of a word, used when the literal meaning will not do. Vedāntins use it to explain “tat tvam asi”: the words point beyond their literal sense. So she is the implied meaning behind all words, not their literal object. Labdhakāmā, whose wishes are already fulfilled, means she acts for others, not out of need.',
 'Some truths are only reached by pointing beyond the words. Learn to read past them.',
 'The meaning <b>behind</b> the words.'),
X(16,'MOTHER OF GAṆEŚA',LS('Lalita with a small Ganesha at her side','LAMBODARA','gift'),
 'Whose forehead shines with a tilaka; adorned with hanging strings of pearls; mother of Lambodara (Gaṇeśa); the attainable; rich in modesty; beyond dissolution.',
 [('lalāmarājadalikā','<b>whose forehead shines with a mark</b>'),('lambimuktālatāñcitā','<b>adorned with hanging strands of pearls</b>'),('lambodaraprasūḥ','<b>mother of the pot-bellied (Gaṇeśa)</b>'),('labhyā','<b>who can be attained</b>'),('lajjāḍhyā','<b>full of modesty</b>'),('layavarjitā','<b>untouched by dissolution</b>')],
 'In the Lalitopākhyāna, when Bhaṇḍa’s general set up a magic obstacle (vighna yantra) to stop Lalitā’s army, she glanced at Kāmeśvara, laughed, and from her laughter Gaṇeśa was born; he destroyed the obstacle. This is why she is Lambodaraprasū.',
 'Labhyā, attainable, is a consoling name after so much that is beyond words: she can be reached. Lajjā, modesty or shyness, is also the name of a seed-syllable in Śākta tradition, which the next group of names makes central. Laya is dissolution, the end of a world-age; she remains through it, as verse 12 said.',
 'What seems beyond reach can still be reached. The hymn insists on it.',
 'She can be <b>reached</b>.'),
X(17,'THE SEED SOUND',LS('Lalita inside a glowing seed-syllable','HRĪṂ','om'),
 'Whose form is hrīṃ; who dwells in hrīṃ; who loves the word hrīṃ; whose seed is hrīṃ; whose mantra is hrīṃ; whose mark is hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkārarūpā','<b>whose form is the syllable hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāranilayā','<b>who dwells in hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃpadapriyā','<b>who loves the word hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārabījā','<b>whose seed is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāramantrā','<b>whose mantra is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāralakṣaṇā','<b>whose sign is hrīṃ</b>')],
 'Three of the fifteen groups in the Triśatī are made of hrīṃ names, sixty names in all. Hrīṃ is the bīja (seed syllable) of the Goddess known as the bhuvaneśvarī bīja or the māyā bīja; it is widely used in Devī worship and appears in many published hymns. We explain its meaning but not its ritual use, which belongs to a teacher.',
 'Traditional analyses of hrīṃ break it into sounds: h for space or Śiva, r for fire or energy, ī for the Goddess’s power, and the nasal ṃ for the point beyond sound. The name lists that follow are almost incantatory: the same seed turned this way and that, until the listener’s mind rests in it. Hrī also means modesty, linking to lajjā in the previous verse.',
 'Repetition steadies the mind. Find a word that holds yours.',
 'All in one <b>seed</b>.'),
X(18,'DELIGHTED BY THE SEED',LS('Lalita adorned with a seed-syllable jewel','HRĪṂMATĪ','gift'),
 'Delighted by the repetition of hrīṃ; possessed of hrīṃ; adorned with hrīṃ; whose nature is hrīṃ; worshipped by the word hrīṃ; whose womb is hrīṃ; whose name is the word hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkārajapasuprītā','<b>pleased by the japa of hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃmatī','<b>possessing hrīṃ, or modesty</b>'),('hrīṃvibhūṣaṇā','<b>whose ornament is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃśīlā','<b>whose character is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃpadārādhyā','<b>worshipped by the word hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃgarbhā','<b>who holds hrīṃ within</b>')],
 'This verse lists seven names in two lines; the seventh, hrīṃpadābhidhā, “whose name is hrīṃ”, closes it. Many names here can be read two ways, because hrī means modesty and hrīṃ is the seed syllable.',
 'Hrīṃmatī, for instance, is both “she who has the hrīṃ syllable” and “she who is modest”; hrīṃśīlā is both “whose nature is the seed” and “whose character is modesty”. The double sense is intended: the Goddess’s power is clothed in gentleness. Japa, quiet repetition, is the main practice associated with seed-syllables, and the first name says such practice pleases her.',
 'Strength wearing modesty is the most beautiful kind.',
 'Power clothed in <b>modesty</b>.'),
X(19,'THE SEAT OF THE SEED',LS('Lalita seated on a throne marked with a seed-syllable','PĪṬHIKĀ','crown'),
 'Expressed by hrīṃ; worshipped through hrīṃ; whose seat is hrīṃ; known by hrīṃ; contemplated through hrīṃ; hrīṃ itself; whose body is hrīṃ.',
 [('hrīṃkāravācyā','<b>the meaning expressed by hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārapūjyā','<b>worshipped through hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkārapīṭhikā','<b>whose seat is hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāravedyā','<b>to be known through hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃkāracintyā','<b>to be contemplated through hrīṃ</b>'),('hrīṃ · hrīṃśarīriṇī','<b>hrīṃ itself; whose body is hrīṃ</b>')],
 'This verse completes the first group of hrīṃ names and with it the first five groups, a hundred names. Traditional texts sometimes treat these five groups as one section of the larger scheme.',
 'The progression is philosophical: the syllable expresses her (vācya), she is worshipped through it (pūjya), sits on it (pīṭhikā), is known through it (vedya), contemplated through it (cintya), and finally is it, and has it as her body. Sound and its meaning become one. This is a central teaching of mantra-śāstra: the word, rightly held, is the presence it names.',
 'Words can become what they mean when we live them.',
 'The word <b>is</b> her.'),
X(20,'THE DEER-EYED',LS('Lalita with deer-like eyes, Brahma, Vishnu and Indra bowing','HARIṆEKṢAṆĀ','gods'),
 'Whose form is the syllable ha; worshipped by the plough-bearer; doe-eyed; beloved of Hara; worshipped by Hara; saluted by Hari, Brahmā and Indra.',
 [('hakārarūpā','<b>whose form is the syllable ha</b>'),('haladhṛkpūjitā','<b>worshipped by the plough-bearer (Balarāma)</b>'),('hariṇekṣaṇā','<b>whose eyes are like a doe’s</b>'),('harapriyā','<b>beloved of Hara (Śiva)</b>'),('harārādhyā','<b>worshipped by Hara</b>'),('haribrahmendravanditā','<b>saluted by Viṣṇu, Brahmā and Indra</b>')],
 'A new group of twenty begins with ha. The second hundred of names opens here, and many of its names play on hari and hara, Viṣṇu and Śiva.',
 'Haladhṛk, the bearer of the plough, is Balarāma, Kṛṣṇa’s elder brother. Some commentators read the name more broadly as “worshipped by farmers”. Harapriyā and harārādhyā put two relations side by side: she is Śiva’s beloved and the one he worships. The doe’s eyes, large and gentle, are a classical image of beauty in Sanskrit poetry.',
 'Love and reverence can live together in the same relationship.',
 '<b>Beloved</b> and <b>worshipped</b> by Śiva.'),
]
